  "response": "Liang Bua is a limestone cave on the Indonesian island of Flores, and its sediments have yielded stone artifacts, the bones of dwarfed Stegodon, giant rats and Komodo dragons alongside the hominin remains that provoked one of the sharpest disputes in recent paleoanthropology. Work in the cave has proceeded over many seasons, with trenches cut through several meters of finely stratified deposit. The skeleton designated LB1 was excavated at Liang Bua in 2003 by a joint Australian-Indonesian team, a recovery that came after LB1 was first discovered at Liang Bua in 1994. Only once that 2003 excavation had brought the skeleton out of the ground could Peter Brown and colleagues describe LB1 in the journal Nature in 2004. The taxonomic step depended in turn on that paper: only with the description in print did the describing team assign the Liang Bua fossils to the new species Homo floresiensis.\n\nThat assignment drew an immediate rebuttal. Because the new species had been named, Indonesian paleoanthropologist Teuku Jacob argued that LB1 was a modern human with microcephaly, a disorder that produces an abnormally small brain and skull. His argument faced an awkward fact, even though he pressed it forcefully: LB1 had a cranial capacity of about 900 cubic centimeters. What brought the dispute over the skull to a close was a direct study of brain form. Dean Falk and colleagues concluded from virtual endocast comparisons that LB1's brain shape did not match that of microcephalic modern humans. Their reconstructions compared LB1 with the endocasts of normal humans, of microcephalic individuals and of fossil hominins, and the shape of the frontal and temporal regions, rather than raw volume, carried the comparison. With that result, the microcephaly diagnosis no longer had a morphological basis.\n\nA second line of argument concerned the size of the sample. A pathological explanation is far easier to sustain for a single aberrant skeleton than for a population, so the number of individuals in the cave bore directly on the question. Either LB1 is the only hominin individual represented in the Liang Bua deposits, or the Liang Bua deposits contain remains of more than one hominin individual; the two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Excavators catalogued teeth, mandibles, arm bones and leg bones from successive layers, and each element was examined for duplication of anatomical parts, the standard test for counting individuals in a fossil assemblage.\n\nPhylogenetic work then placed the Flores hominin on the tree of the genus. The 2017 phylogenetic analysis by Debbie Argue and colleagues concluded that Homo floresiensis is a distinct species most likely descended from an early Homo lineage. Because of that analysis, Homo floresiensis is a hominin species distinct from Homo sapiens. In other words, the Liang Bua fossils belong to a species other than modern humans. The coded characters in such a study span the skull, jaw, teeth and postcranial skeleton, so the placement does not hinge on any single anatomical region.\n\nThe remaining questions concern how the lineage arrived on the island. Whereas the cave holds those several individuals, the ancestors of Homo floresiensis reached Flores by crossing open water, or else the ancestors of Homo floresiensis reached Flores over a land connection exposed during low sea levels; at least one of these routes was used, perhaps both. Flores lies east of the Wallace Line, and its fauna bears the marks of long isolation, which is why the route of colonization remains a central topic for researchers studying the Flores hominin. The debate over pathology itself, however, has been closed by the convergence of endocast, skeletal and phylogenetic evidence.",
